    "fullText": "The Ramayana\r\n    \r\n        body {\r\n            font-family: 'Georgia', serif;\r\n            background-color: #fdf5e6;\r\n            color: #3a241d;\r\n            line-height: 1.6;\r\n            margin: 0;\r\n            padding: 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        header {\r\n            background-color: #d4af37;\r\n            color: #fff;\r\n            text-align: center;\r\n            padding: 20px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        h1, h2 {\r\n            color: #a85a32;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        h1 {\r\n            font-size: 2em;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        .content {\r\n            max-width: 800px;\r\n            margin: 20px auto;\r\n            padding: 20px;\r\n            background-color: #fff7e6;\r\n            box-shadow: 0px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);\r\n            border-radius: 8px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        .section {\r\n            margin-bottom: 20px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        .section h2 {\r\n            font-size: 1.5em;\r\n            border-bottom: 2px solid #d4af37;\r\n            padding-bottom: 5px;\r\n            margin-bottom: 10px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        ul {\r\n            list-style-type: none;\r\n            padding: 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        li {\r\n            background-color: #f7dec3;\r\n            padding: 10px;\r\n            border-radius: 5px;\r\n            margin: 5px 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        footer {\r\n            text-align: center;\r\n            padding: 10px;\r\n            background-color: #a85a32;\r\n            color: #fff;\r\n            font-size: 0.9em;\r\n        }\r\n    \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    The Ramayana\r\n    An Epic of Virtue, Valor, and Devotion\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n        Introduction\r\n        The Ramayana is one of the greatest epics of ancient Indian literature, attributed to the sage Valmiki. This timeless story, told over 24,000 verses, follows the life of Prince Rama, his exile, the abduction of his wife Sita, and his battle to rescue her. The Ramayana not only entertains but also conveys profound moral and spiritual lessons.\r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Structure of the Ramayana\r\n        The Ramayana is traditionally divided into seven books or Kandas:\r\n        \r\n            Bala Kanda: Childhood of Rama and his early life.\r\n            Ayodhya Kanda: Rama’s exile and the journey into the forest.\r\n            Aranya Kanda: Forest adventures and Sita’s abduction by Ravana.\r\n            Kishkindha Kanda: Rama's alliance with Hanuman and Sugriva.\r\n            Sundara Kanda: Hanuman’s journey to Lanka and Sita’s discovery.\r\n            Yuddha Kanda: The epic battle in Lanka and Sita’s rescue.\r\n            Uttara Kanda: Events following Rama’s return to Ayodhya, including Sita's departure.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Main Characters\r\n        \r\n            Rama: The hero and seventh incarnation of Lord Vishnu, embodying righteousness and virtue.\r\n            Sita: Rama's wife, known for her purity, devotion, and resilience.\r\n            Hanuman: The loyal devotee of Rama, symbolizing strength, devotion, and intelligence.\r\n            Lakshmana: Rama’s devoted brother who accompanies him into exile.\r\n            Ravana: The ten-headed demon king of Lanka who abducts Sita, representing ego and lust.\r\n            Bharata: Rama’s brother who rules Ayodhya in his absence, symbolizing loyalty and humility.\r\n            Dasharatha: The King of Ayodhya and father of Rama.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Summary of Key Events\r\n        The Ramayana follows a captivating sequence of events that weave together heroism, devotion, and the triumph of good over evil:\r\n        \r\n            Exile of Rama: Due to palace intrigues, Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana are exiled for 14 years.\r\n            Abduction of Sita: In the forest, Sita is abducted by Ravana and taken to Lanka.\r\n            Search for Sita: Rama allies with Hanuman and the vanara (monkey) army to find Sita.\r\n            Hanuman's Journey: Hanuman leaps across the ocean to Lanka, discovers Sita, and brings hope to her.\r\n            The Great Battle: Rama’s army confronts Ravana’s forces in an epic war, culminating in Ravana’s defeat.\r\n            Return to Ayodhya: Rama and Sita return to Ayodhya, marking the joyous festival of Diwali.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Themes of the Ramayana\r\n        The Ramayana emphasizes various values and themes:\r\n        \r\n            Dharma (Righteousness): Rama’s actions reflect his unwavering commitment to dharma.\r\n            Devotion and Loyalty: Hanuman’s devotion, Lakshmana’s loyalty, and Sita’s dedication are exemplary.\r\n            Good vs. Evil: The battle between Rama and Ravana symbolizes the victory of good over evil.\r\n            Compassion and Forgiveness: Rama’s kindness and forgiveness toward enemies display his compassion.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    &copy; 2023 - An Exploration of the Ramayana"
