    "fullText": "The Mahabharata\r\n    \r\n        body {\r\n            font-family: 'Georgia', serif;\r\n            background-color: #f4e3b2;\r\n            color: #4b2a2a;\r\n            line-height: 1.6;\r\n            margin: 0;\r\n            padding: 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        header {\r\n            background-color: #a85a32;\r\n            color: #fff;\r\n            text-align: center;\r\n            padding: 20px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        h1, h2 {\r\n            color: #a85a32;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        .content {\r\n            max-width: 800px;\r\n            margin: 20px auto;\r\n            padding: 20px;\r\n            background-color: #fff8e7;\r\n            box-shadow: 0px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);\r\n            border-radius: 8px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        .section {\r\n            margin-bottom: 20px;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        ul {\r\n            list-style-type: none;\r\n            padding: 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        li {\r\n            background-color: #f7dec3;\r\n            padding: 10px;\r\n            border-radius: 5px;\r\n            margin: 5px 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        footer {\r\n            text-align: center;\r\n            padding: 10px;\r\n            background-color: #a85a32;\r\n            color: #fff;\r\n            font-size: 0.9em;\r\n        }\r\n    \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    The Mahabharata\r\n    An Epic Tale of Dharma, Duty, and Devotion\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n        Introduction\r\n        The Mahabharata is one of the two major Sanskrit epics of ancient Indian history, the other being the Ramayana. Written by the sage Vyasa, it is the longest epic poem in the world, containing approximately 100,000 verses, detailing the story of the Kurukshetra War, the conflict between two royal families, the Pandavas and Kauravas.\r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Main Plot\r\n        The Mahabharata revolves around the dynastic struggle between the Pandavas and the Kauravas. The Pandavas, sons of King Pandu, are rightful heirs to the throne of Hastinapura, but their cousins, the Kauravas, led by Duryodhana, refuse to acknowledge their claim. This conflict culminates in the great Kurukshetra War, a battle that symbolizes the clash between righteousness and unrighteousness, justice and injustice.\r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Key Characters\r\n        \r\n            Krishna: The divine incarnation and charioteer of Arjuna who provides spiritual guidance in the Bhagavad Gita.\r\n            Yudhishthira: The eldest Pandava, known for his righteousness and adherence to dharma.\r\n            Bhima: Known for his immense strength, he is the second Pandava.\r\n            Arjuna: The skilled archer, third Pandava, and recipient of the Bhagavad Gita's teachings.\r\n            Draupadi: The wife of the Pandavas, a pivotal character whose humiliation leads to the final conflict.\r\n            Duryodhana: The leader of the Kauravas, driven by ambition and rivalry with the Pandavas.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Major Themes\r\n        \r\n            Dharma (Righteousness): The struggle between upholding dharma and succumbing to adharma (unrighteousness) is central to the Mahabharata.\r\n            Family and Duty: The complex relationships among family members raise questions of loyalty, justice, and duty.\r\n            Divine Intervention: Krishna’s role as both a god and a friend emphasizes the concept of divine will guiding human affairs.\r\n            Morality and Ethics: The Mahabharata explores moral dilemmas, with characters often torn between right and wrong, good and evil.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        The Bhagavad Gita\r\n        One of the most sacred texts of Hinduism, the Bhagavad Gita forms a part of the Mahabharata. In this spiritual discourse, Krishna enlightens Arjuna on his duty, karma, and the paths to self-realization. The Gita has been revered for its teachings on life, purpose, and the nature of reality, and continues to inspire millions worldwide.\r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Other Notable Episodes\r\n        \r\n            The Game of Dice: A pivotal event where Yudhishthira loses his kingdom and Draupadi is humiliated, setting the stage for the Kurukshetra War.\r\n            The Exile: The Pandavas are forced into exile for 13 years, during which they encounter various adventures and hardships.\r\n            The War of Kurukshetra: A climactic battle lasting 18 days, where both sides face immense loss, leading to a profound transformation of characters.\r\n            The Ashwamedha Yajna: After the war, Yudhishthira performs the Ashwamedha sacrifice to establish peace and consolidate his rule.\r\n        \r\n    \r\n\r\n    \r\n        Conclusion\r\n        The Mahabharata is not just a story of war and conflict but a vast compendium of knowledge encompassing philosophy, ethics, and spirituality. Its teachings are timeless, encouraging readers to reflect on the moral dilemmas of life, the pursuit of dharma, and the purpose of existence.\r\n    \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    &copy; 2023 - A Tribute to the Epic Mahabharata"
